=====Usage notes=====
* Syntactically, the adverb ''there'' fulfills two functions:
** As a [[demonstrative]] or [[exophoric]] adverb it is typically accompanied by a gesture of pointing to a particular location.
**: {{ux|en|—Have you seen my glasses?<br>—Why, they're right '''there'''!}}
** As an [[anaphoric]] adverb it is used to refer to a location that was previously mentioned.
**: {{ux|en|—Have you been to London?<br>—No, but I've always wanted to go '''there'''.}}
**: {{ux|en|''Souse'' forms prevail across all of the South, except in the coastal regions. '''There''', ''[[hoghead]] cheese'' and ''hog's [[head cheese]]'' are mainly used.}}
* The use of ''there'' instead of ''[[they're]]'' (meaning ''they are'') or ''[[their]]'' (possessive form of ''they'') is a common {{l|en|homophone#Noun|homophonic}} error in English writing.
* {{sense|to or into that place}}
** ''There'' is sometimes used by way of exclamation, calling attention to something, especially to something distant; such as in the phrases {{uxi|en|'''There, there'''!}}, {{uxi|en|See '''there'''!}} and {{uxi|en|Look '''there'''!}}
** ''There'' is often used as an expletive, and in this use, when it introduces a sentence or clause, the verb precedes its subject.
**: {{ux|en|'''There''' remains to be decided what we do with the dog.}}
** ''There'' is much used in composition, and often has the sense of a pronoun. See {{m|en|thereabout}}, {{m|en|thereafter}}, {{m|en|therefrom}}, etc.

